# {{ config.project.name }}

Generated by [`0-mcp init`](https://github.com/ssjunior/0-mcp). REST API,
OpenAPI docs and an MCP server, all wired up to your existing
{{ config.project.backend }} database.

## Quick start

```bash
./run.sh
```

The script creates a `uv` virtual environment on first run, installs
dependencies, copies `.env.example` to `.env` if missing, and starts
the dev server. On subsequent runs it skips the install steps and just
boots the server.

If you don't have `uv` yet:

```bash
curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh
```

## What you get at `http://localhost:8000`

- `/` — index of available endpoints.
- `/docs` — interactive REST docs (Scalar UI).
- `/openapi.json` — OpenAPI 3.1 spec, ready for codegen.
- `/mcp/tools` — agent-facing tool registry, browseable.
- `/mcp/tools.json` — same registry as JSON.
- `/mcp` — JSON-RPC 2.0 endpoint (POST). Agents call tools through here.

## Project layout

```
.
├── manage.py / asgi.py
├── settings/
│   ├── env.py            # auto-loads .env on import
│   └── settings.py       # MCP settings + INSTALLED_APPS
├── router/
│   ├── endpoints.py      # {ClassName}Resource → URL pattern map
│   └── urls.py
└── modules/
    └── <prefix>/
        ├── apps.py
        ├── models.py     # one Django model per table
        └── resources.py  # one BaseResource per model
```

Tables sharing a name prefix land in the same module —
`contract`, `contract_item`, `contract_history` all live in
`modules/contract/`.

## Customizing resources

Generated resources are intentionally minimal:

```python
class ClientResource(BaseResource):
    model = Client
```

The framework's defaults handle CRUD, pagination, search, ordering and
MCP tool generation. Add per-resource opinions when you need them:

```python
class ClientResource(BaseResource):
    model = Client
    filter_fields = ['name', 'active', 'type_id']
    search_fields = ['name', 'email']
    mcp_fk_expand = {
        'type': ['id', 'name'],
        'currency': ['id', 'code'],
    }
```

See the [0-mcp BaseResource reference](https://github.com/ssjunior/0-mcp)
for every available knob.

## Re-running the generator

If your database schema changes, re-run:

```bash
0-mcp init --db {{ config.project.backend }}://... --output {{ config.project.name }}
```

Generated files are overwritten — keep your customizations either in
subclasses outside `modules/<prefix>/` or version-control the changes
so you can re-apply them.

## Production checklist

The generated project is **demo-friendly by default** so the first
`./run.sh` boots without ceremony. Before deploying anywhere
internet-facing, work through this list:

1. **`.env`** — flip `DEBUG=false`, set `ALLOWED_HOSTS=app.example.com,…`,
   rotate `DJANGO_SECRET_KEY`.
2. **`router/urls.py`** — replace the demo block with
   ```python
   urlpatterns = get_routes(endpoints, mcp=True, docs_public=False)
   ```
   That single line gates `/mcp`, `/docs` and `/openapi.json` behind the
   same X-Api-Key/session as every REST resource.
3. **`settings/settings.py`** — flip `'DEFAULT_AUTHENTICATED': True`
   in the `MCP` dict so every REST resource starts requiring auth.
   Set `ENFORCE_TOKEN`, `TRUSTED_PROXIES`, `ALLOWED_ORIGINS` if you
   serve a browser frontend on a different domain.
4. **`TENANT_USER_API_MODEL`** — point at the model that stores your
   API keys (column expected: `api_key`). Without it, MCP/REST traffic
   has no way to authenticate.

## Schema ownership
{% if config.project.django_managed %}
This project was generated with `--django-managed`. Models drop
`Meta.managed = False`, and `./run.sh` runs
`makemigrations` + `migrate --fake-initial` on first boot — Django
records the existing tables as already migrated and from then on you
evolve the schema the standard way (`makemigrations` + `migrate`).
{% else %}
This project was generated in **read-only** mode (the default for
introspection). Every model has `Meta.managed = False`, so Django
reads but never issues DDL — your existing migration tool stays in
charge of the schema. To switch to Django-managed:

1. Re-run `0-mcp init` with `--django-managed --writable`, **or**
2. Delete `Meta.managed = False` lines you want Django to own and run
   `python manage.py makemigrations && python manage.py migrate --fake-initial` once.
{% endif %}

## Auto-applied opinions

The generator pre-populates a few things to keep you safe:

- **Sensitive columns** (`password`, `token`, `secret`, `api_key`,
  `otp`, `*_password`, `*_token`, …) flagged in `mcp_exclude_fields`
  so agents never see them.
- **Django system tables** (`django_*`, `auth_*`) marked
  `expose: false` — they don't appear in MCP/REST until you
  flip the flag.
- **MySQL `tinyint(1)`** mapped to `BooleanField`.
- **Foreign keys** qualified with `'app.Model'` so cross-module
  references resolve.
- **Composite primary keys** emitted via
  `models.CompositePrimaryKey` (Django 5.2+).

## Troubleshooting

- **`KeyError: 'DB_NAME'`** → no `.env` file. Run `cp .env.example
  .env` and edit the credentials.
- **`Blocked due to misbehavior`** → too many failed requests in a
  row triggered the rate limiter; with `DEBUG=true` this should never
  happen, but if it does run `redis-cli del "{{ config.project.name }}:rate_limit:blocked:127.0.0.1"`.
- **`Apps aren't loaded yet`** → import order issue. Ensure scripts
  start with `from zeromcp import orm` before any model import, or
  set `DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE=settings.settings` first.

